* Where to Go from here...


The
[[⟪GTI18N_45ef131e_000001⟫][Go Documentation]] is a great place to
start.
It contains references, tutorials, videos, and more.

To learn how to organize and work with Go code, read [[⟪GTI18N_45ef131e_000002⟫][How to Write Go Code]].

If you need help with the standard library, see the [[⟪GTI18N_45ef131e_000003⟫][package reference]]. For help with the language itself, you might be surprised to find the [[⟪GTI18N_45ef131e_000004⟫][Language Spec]] is quite readable.

To further explore Go's concurrency model, watch
[[⟪GTI18N_45ef131e_000005⟫][Go Concurrency Patterns]]
([[⟪GTI18N_45ef131e_000006⟫][slides]])
and
[[⟪GTI18N_45ef131e_000007⟫][Advanced Go Concurrency Patterns]]
([[⟪GTI18N_45ef131e_000008⟫][slides]])
and read the
[[⟪GTI18N_45ef131e_000009⟫][Share Memory by Communicating]]
codewalk.

To get started writing web applications, watch
[[⟪GTI18N_45ef131e_000010⟫][A simple programming environment]]
([[⟪GTI18N_45ef131e_000011⟫][slides]])
and read the
[[⟪GTI18N_45ef131e_000012⟫][Writing Web Applications]] tutorial.

The [[⟪GTI18N_45ef131e_000013⟫][First Class Functions in Go]] codewalk gives an interesting perspective on Go's function types.

The [[⟪GTI18N_45ef131e_000014⟫][Go Blog]] has a large archive of informative Go articles.

Visit [[⟪GTI18N_45ef131e_000015⟫][the Go home page]] for more.
